<div>
  <section class="catalogue-intro">
    <h2>A shared language for everyday work.</h2><p>
      Compact, legible components that keep business information together. These examples use the same Phoenix components that customer workflows can compose.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="catalogue-grid">
    <section class="catalogue-panel">
      <h2>Semantic colour</h2><div class="catalogue-swatches">
        <div :for={role <- ~w(ink accent paper canvas line)}>
          <div class={"swatch swatch-#{role}"}></div><strong>{role}</strong>
        </div>
      </div><p>
        Forest emphasis, warm surfaces, explicit labels. Try the theme switch: colour roles change, meaning stays the same.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="catalogue-panel">
      <h2>Type & density</h2><h3 class="catalogue-type">200A panel replacement</h3><p>
        Working text · Barlow 14px
      </p><small>Supporting context · 12px</small><div class="catalogue-rule"></div><p>
        32px controls · 44px standard rows · 34px compact rows · 44px mobile controls
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="catalogue-panel">
      <h2>Behaviour is part of the contract</h2><p>
        Focusable controls, recoverable validation, preserved input, and honest state are library responsibilities.
      </p><.button id="exercise-control" phx-click="exercise" class="wb-button">Exercise control</.button><p
        id="exercise-result"
        role="status"
      >
        Activated {@count} times
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="catalogue-panel">
      <h2>What ships with a component</h2><ul>
        <li>Named attributes and slots</li><li>Keyboard and responsive behaviour</li><li>
          Empty, error, success and disabled states
        </li><li>Examples and behaviour tests</li>
      </ul><p>Presentation components do not own permissions or business writes.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
